{{!-- One numbered inventory line, plus its Fatigue circle.

      Three shapes: empty, a continuation of the item above, or an item — a sack of coin is an
      item like any other (`data/item-coin.js`), its value on its tag. A multi-slot
      item is drawn as ONE block as tall as the slots it fills — its first row carries the name,
      its continuations carry nothing, and a drawn bracket in the gutter runs the height of the
      pair. The block used to be a band of `--cairn-paper-sunk` instead, which is the one fill
      this sheet permits and was being spent here on a mark the eye reads as a highlight rather
      than as a join. The bracket is the mark the printed sheet uses, and it is drawn in the same
      ink as every other rule on the page.

      The row expands into nothing. It used to be a `<details>`: clicking the name unfolded the
      item's description under it, which pushed every row below it down the ledger and moved the
      numbered lines the layout is built on. The name opens the item's own sheet instead, and the
      description is posted to chat from the scroll control — so the ten lines never move.

      The circle is inert on an occupied slot: Fatigue cannot be marked over something carried.

      `draggable` is a CLASS, not the HTML attribute. v14 binds dragging through
      `DragDrop({ dragSelector: ".draggable" })` on every sheet (`ux/drag-drop.mjs`), and it sets
      the attribute itself on what it finds. A hand-written `draggable="true"` makes the browser
      start a drag the sheet knows nothing about, carrying an empty `dataTransfer` — which every
      drop target then refuses in silence. A continuation row is the same item drawn again, so it
      is not a second drag source. --}}
<li class="cairn-slot{{#if row.empty}} is-empty{{/if}}{{#if row.continuation}} is-continuation{{/if}}{{#if row.item.isUsedUp}} used-up{{/if}}{{#if (gt row.spans 1)}} is-block{{/if}}{{#if row.item}}{{#unless row.continuation}} draggable{{/unless}}{{/if}}"
    {{!-- No rule under the last slot: the ledger's own frame already closes it. --}}
    {{!-- ...and none between two rows of the same item: that is the one place the ledger's lines
          would cut a single entry in half. `joined` is false on the block's LAST row, so the
          rule that separates the block from the next thing carried is still drawn. --}}
    {{#unless row.last}}{{#unless row.joined}}data-ink="hline" data-ink-seed="slot-{{row.n}}" data-ink-w="0.9" data-ink-a="0.42" data-ink-at="bottom"{{/unless}}{{/unless}}
    {{#if (gt row.spans 1)}}data-spans="{{row.spans}}"{{/if}}
    {{#if row.item}}data-item-id="{{row.item.id}}"{{/if}}>

    <span class="cairn-slot-number">{{row.n}}</span>

    {{!-- The bracket that ties a multi-slot item's rows together, drawn once on the FIRST of
          them and sized in CSS to reach the foot of the last. Its seed is the item's own id, so
          the stroke keeps its silhouette across every re-render of the ledger. --}}
    {{#if (gt row.spans 1)}}{{#unless row.continuation}}
    <span class="cairn-slot-bracket" data-ink="bracket" data-ink-w="1" data-ink-a="0.5"
        data-ink-seed="bracket-{{row.item.id}}"></span>
    {{/unless}}{{/if}}

    {{#if row.empty}}
    <span class="cairn-slot-rule"></span>

    {{else if row.continuation}}
    <span class="cairn-slot-body"></span>

    {{else}}
    <span class="cairn-slot-body">
        {{!-- The name IS the affordance, as it was when this was a disclosure — only now it opens
              the item rather than unfolding it. `itemEdit` is that handler already, so the row
              and the pencil are one action and cannot drift apart. It is a sibling of the
              controls and never their ancestor, so a click on a control is that control's and
              nothing else's. The use pips are the one control it does contain, and they are
              safe because core dispatches to the innermost `data-action` only. A Fatigue opens
              too: it carries no numbers, but it carries a description, and "which Fatigue is
              this one" is a note worth writing. --}}
        <span class="cairn-item-name-and-tags" data-action="itemEdit">
            <span class="cairn-item-title">
                {{#if row.item.system.equipped}}<strong>{{row.item.name}}</strong>{{else}}{{row.item.name}}{{/if}}
            </span>
            {{> "systems/cairn2e/templates/parts/item-tags.hbs" system=row.item.system}}
            {{!-- A container on the ten (a Cart you pull, a bag) carries its capacity chip here
                  too. Its contents are read on its own sheet's Contents tab: this row cannot
                  unfold without moving the numbered lines under it. --}}
            {{#if row.item.system.isContainer}}<span class="cairn-item-tag">{{row.item.system.contentsSlots}}/{{row.item.system.capacity}}</span>{{/if}}
            {{#if row.item.showUses}}{{> "systems/cairn2e/templates/parts/item-uses.hbs" uses=row.item.usesDisplay editable=@root.editable}}{{/if}}
        </span>

        {{> "systems/cairn2e/templates/parts/item-controls.hbs" item=row.item editable=@root.editable}}
    </span>
    {{/if}}

    {{!-- On a sheet the user may not write, the circle is the mark and not the control: a marked
          one still says a Fatigue is there. --}}
    {{#if row.fatigue.inert}}
    <span class="cairn-fatigue-dot is-inert"></span>
    {{else if @root.editable}}
    <a class="cairn-fatigue-dot{{#if row.fatigue.marked}} is-marked{{/if}}" data-action="fatigueToggle"
        data-tooltip="{{#if row.fatigue.marked}}{{localize 'CAIRN.RemoveFatigue'}}{{else}}{{localize 'CAIRN.AddFatigue'}}{{/if}}"></a>
    {{else}}
    <span class="cairn-fatigue-dot {{#if row.fatigue.marked}}is-marked{{else}}is-inert{{/if}}"></span>
    {{/if}}
</li>
